<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <parent>
    <groupId>dev.mainframe</groupId>
    <artifactId>mainframe-parent</artifactId>
    <version>0.1.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
  </parent>

  <artifactId>mainframe-template</artifactId>
  <packaging>jar</packaging>

  <name>MainFrame Templates</name>
  <description>Starting a project from a template: new vexel-desktop</description>

  <!--
    The adapter, and only the adapter. This module used to hold two packages:

      dev.mainframe.template        the engine. Reads a template, works out what
                                    files it would write, and refuses the ones it
                                    should not. Imported nothing from MainFrame.
      dev.mainframe.template.shell  the adapter. Turns slots into MainFrame form
                                    fields, answers into a record, and the result
                                    into a Plan, so `new` gets the same dry-run,
                                    the same confirmation and the same errors as
                                    every other command that writes.

    The could-another-project-use-it test was asked of the first package, and it
    answered itself: the engine moved to vexelray-framework-template, with the
    vexel-desktop template and its native-image metadata, because what the builder
    emits is that framework's primary witness. It moved unchanged apart from its
    package name, which is the evidence the boundary was real. What is left here
    is the join, which was never meant to be reusable.

    vexelray-framework-template is JDK-only, like the engine always was, so this
    module still builds on a machine with no GPU. It is a local install: run
    `mvn install` in vexelray-framework first.
  -->
  <dependencies>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>dev.mainframe</groupId>
      <artifactId>mainframe-core</artifactId>
      <version>${project.version}</version>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>dev.vexelray.framework</groupId>
      <artifactId>vexelray-framework-template</artifactId>
      <version>${vexelray-framework.version}</version>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
      <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>
</project>
